{
  "severity": "critical",
  "network_observations": [
    {"ip": "10.30.6.81", "inferred_role": "attacker_workstation_internal_source", "first_seen": "2028-01-19T01:48:42+0100", "last_seen": "2028-01-19T02:02:18+0100", "agent_name": "WS-IT-32"},
    {"ip": "10.30.5.41", "inferred_role": "target_file_server_kerberos_victim", "first_seen": "2028-01-19T01:58:44+0100", "last_seen": "2028-01-19T02:02:18+0100", "agent_name": "FS01"},
    {"ip": "10.30.2.10", "inferred_role": "domain_controller_BYPASSED_no_kdc_contact", "first_seen": "2028-01-19T01:58:44+0100", "last_seen": "2028-01-19T02:02:18+0100", "agent_name": "DC-CORP-01"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Credential Access / Forge Kerberos Ticket", "technique": "T1558.002", "what_happened": "At 2028-01-19 01:48:42 CET, Sysmon Event 1 fired on WS-IT-32.corp.local (10.30.6.81) under user=CORP\\local-admin: image=mimikatz.exe, parentImage=cmd.exe, commandLine='mimikatz.exe \"kerberos::silver /target:FS01.corp.local /service:cifs /rc4:e4f8c2b1d9e7c8d3a4b5c6d7e8f9a0b1 /user:svc_backup /id:500 /domain:corp.local /ptt\"'. The mimikatz 'kerberos::silver' module forges a Kerberos service ticket (TGS) directly — NOT a TGT. Unlike a Golden Ticket (which requires the krbtgt account hash and creates a TGT usable across the domain), a Silver Ticket requires only the SERVICE ACCOUNT's NTLM/RC4 hash and produces a TGS for one specific service. The RC4 hash 'e4f8c2b1d9e7c8d3a4b5c6d7e8f9a0b1' is the cifs/FS01 service account (svc_backup) hash — previously extracted (probably via LSASS dump on a prior victim). The '/ptt' flag pass-the-tickets the forged TGS directly into the current logon session's Kerberos ticket cache for immediate use.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=mimikatz.exe + commandLine contains 'kerberos::silver' + '/target:FS01' + '/service:cifs' + '/rc4:' + '/ptt'. Diagnostic of Silver Ticket forgery — the kerberos::silver verb is unique to mimikatz and indicates the attacker possesses a service account hash and is forging a TGS to impersonate svc_backup against FS01's cifs service.", "first_seen": "2028-01-19T01:48:42+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Lateral Movement / Use Forged Ticket — KDC BYPASSED", "technique": "T1558.002 + T1021.002", "what_happened": "At 01:58:44 CET, a Wazuh custom decoder on FS01.corp.local (10.30.5.41) fired KERBEROS_AUTH event with service=cifs/FS01, user=svc_backup, kdc_contact=false. The kdc_contact=false flag is the KEY DETECTION SIGNAL — Silver Tickets are presented DIRECTLY to the target service (which validates them locally using its own service account key) and NEVER touch the KDC. The 10-minute gap between forgery (01:48:42) and use (01:58:44) is consistent with attacker reconnaissance / staging time on WS-IT-32 between mimikatz execution and ticket presentation.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh custom decoder on FS01: KERBEROS_AUTH event with service=cifs/FS01 + user=svc_backup + kdc_contact=false. The kdc_contact=false signal is the smoking gun for Silver Ticket usage — under normal Kerberos workflow, every service ticket request hits the KDC (TGS-REQ → TGS-REP = Event 4769 on the DC). A Kerberos auth that successfully presents a TGS without any KDC contact is, by definition, a forged ticket.", "first_seen": "2028-01-19T01:58:44+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Initial Access on Target / Successful Kerberos Logon", "technique": "T1078 + T1550.003", "what_happened": "At 01:58:44 CET (same second as the KERBEROS_AUTH event), Windows Security Event 4624 fired on FS01: logonType=3 (Network), TargetUserName=svc_backup, AuthenticationPackageName=Kerberos, IpAddress=10.30.6.81 (WS-IT-32). The attacker is now authenticated to FS01 as svc_backup via the forged TGS — FS01 accepted the ticket as valid because the ticket was correctly encrypted with the cifs/FS01 service account's own key (which the attacker possesses).", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4624 on FS01: TargetUserName=svc_backup + LogonType=3 + AuthenticationPackageName=Kerberos + IpAddress=10.30.6.81. Pairs by timestamp with the kdc_contact=false KERBEROS_AUTH event — confirms the forged ticket was accepted by the target service.", "first_seen": "2028-01-19T01:58:44+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Detection Signal / KDC Cross-Check ABSENT", "technique": "T1558.002 (negative evidence)", "what_happened": "At 01:58:44 CET, a Wazuh decoder cross-check fired: NO matching Event 4769 (Kerberos Service Ticket Request) on DC-CORP-01 for ServiceName=cifs/FS01 + TargetUserName=svc_backup within ±5 minutes of the FS01 4624. Under normal Kerberos workflow, every legitimate svc_backup→cifs/FS01 auth would first produce a 4769 on the DC (the TGS-REQ phase). The complete ABSENCE of any 4769 for this (ServiceName, TargetUserName) pair across the ±5 min window is mathematically inconsistent with legitimate Kerberos — it is the Silver Ticket signature.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh decoder cross-correlation: 4624 logonType=3 Kerberos on FS01 for TargetUserName=svc_backup at T versus 4769 on DC-CORP-01 for ServiceName=cifs/FS01.corp.local + TargetUserName=svc_backup in [T-5min, T+5min] = NO MATCH. Smoking-gun negative-evidence detection: Silver Ticket bypassed the KDC.", "first_seen": "2028-01-19T01:58:44+0100"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Collection / Sensitive File Access", "technique": "T1039 + T1005", "what_happened": "At 01:58:47 CET (3 seconds after the 4624 Kerberos success), Windows Security Event 4663 'An attempt was made to access an object' fired on FS01: ObjectName=\\\\FS01\\Shares\\Finance\\Q3_Strategic.xlsx, AccessList=%%4416,%%4417 (ReadData + WriteData), SubjectUserName=svc_backup. The attacker, authenticated as svc_backup via the forged ticket, is reading AND writing the Q3 Strategic finance file — high-business-impact data.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4663 on FS01: SubjectUserName=svc_backup + ObjectName matching \\\\FS01\\Shares\\Finance\\* + AccessList containing %%4416 (ReadData) + %%4417 (WriteData). Time-locked to 3 seconds after the 4624 Kerberos success = same forged-ticket session.", "first_seen": "2028-01-19T01:58:47+0100"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Collection / Bulk File Access (Data Staging)", "technique": "T1039 + T1074.002", "what_happened": "Between 01:58:47 and 02:02:18 CET (~3.5 minutes), Windows Security generated MULTIPLE Event 4663 on FS01: bulk file access to \\\\FS01\\Shares\\* — the attacker is enumerating + accessing the entire share. This is data-staging behavior consistent with intent to exfiltrate; the file count + access rate substantially exceeds the svc_backup baseline (which normally performs scheduled backup reads at 02:00 / 14:00 only, not interactive reads).", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4663 burst on FS01: SubjectUserName=svc_backup + ObjectName under \\\\FS01\\Shares\\* + high event count within 3.5-min window. The combination of the forged Kerberos session (steps 2-4) and bulk-share access (step 6) is the Silver-Ticket-to-data-collection signature.", "first_seen": "2028-01-19T02:02:18+0100"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2028-01-19T01:48:42+0100", "end": "2028-01-19T02:02:18+0100"},
